extends layouts/_admin.pug

block head
  - var title = "AI Agent Guide"
  - var activePage = "docs"

block content
  .app-title
    div
      h1
        i.bi.bi-robot.me-2
        | AI Agent Guide
      p Use NeoAdmin rules and skills with the tools you already use

    ul.app-breadcrumb.breadcrumb.d-none.d-md-flex
      li.breadcrumb-item
        a(href="docs.html") Documentation
      li.breadcrumb-item.active AI Agent Guide

  .tile
    .tile-body
      .docs.docs-content
        h2.docs-title The Three Parts of Agent Guidance
        p NeoAdmin keeps agent guidance separate from the application UI. The project root contains small tool entry points; the `.agents/` directory contains the reusable rules, technical context, and skills.
        .table-responsive
          table.table.align-middle
            thead
              tr
                th(scope="col") Part
                th(scope="col") Purpose
                th(scope="col") NeoAdmin location
            tbody
              tr
                th(scope="row") Rules
                td Constraints that apply across tasks and limit unnecessary changes
                td <code>.agents/rules/project-standards.md</code>
              tr
                th(scope="row") Context
                td Technical facts the agent should check before changing architecture or build behavior
                td <code>.agents/context/</code>
              tr
                th(scope="row") Skills
                td Focused procedures loaded for a matching task, such as Pug or Sass work
                td <code>.agents/skills/neo-admin-skill-*/SKILL.md</code>
        p.mb-0 Rules are not a task checklist and skills are not installed application code. The agent uses a skill to work through a requested task while the rules constrain how it changes this repository.

        h2.docs-title Start an Agent Session in NeoAdmin
        ol
          li Open the repository root as the workspace so the root instruction file and `.agents/` directory are available.
          li Describe the requested outcome and the page, component, or behavior involved.
          li Ask the agent to use the matching skill when the task is specialized, for example <code>neo-admin-skill-pug</code> for a Pug page or <code>neo-admin-skill-scss</code> for a theme change.
          li Review the source changes and the generated page in <code>docs/</code> after the build.
        p Keep requests specific. For example: <em>“Use neo-admin-skill-bootstrap to align the account actions at Bootstrap’s 768px breakpoint. Preserve the desktop header and the optional bottom navigation, then validate both sides of the breakpoint.”</em>

        h2.docs-title Use the Agent in Your Preferred Tool
        p Keep NeoAdmin's project rules and task workflows in their canonical files. Open the repository root, make the tool aware of <code>AGENTS.md</code>, and ask it to follow the relevant <code>.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md</code>. If the tool requires a native instruction file, use that file as a short pointer to these sources instead of copying their full contents.
        .table-responsive
          table.table.align-middle
            thead
              tr
                th(scope="col") Tool
                th(scope="col") Practical setup
            tbody
              tr
                th(scope="row") Claude Code
                td Open the repository as the working directory. Use a concise root <code>CLAUDE.md</code> pointer to <code>AGENTS.md</code> and the relevant NeoAdmin skill when Claude Code needs native project instructions.
              tr
                th(scope="row") OpenAI Codex
                td Open the repository as the project or workspace. It can use the root <code>AGENTS.md</code> entry point and the linked rules and skills.
              tr
                th(scope="row") Google Antigravity
                td Open the repository as the workspace and use its project rules and skills. Invoke a skill by name when supported, or ask the agent to read its <code>SKILL.md</code>.
              tr
                th(scope="row") Gemini CLI
                td Start the CLI in the repository and provide <code>AGENTS.md</code> as project context. Ask it to read the relevant skill before specialized work.
              tr
                th(scope="row") GitHub Copilot and other tools
                td Configure the tool's native project-instruction mechanism with a short pointer to <code>AGENTS.md</code>, then refer to the matching NeoAdmin skill as needed.
        p.mb-0 Tool features and instruction discovery differ and can change by version. Check each tool's current project-instruction settings; keep NeoAdmin's actual standards in one canonical place.

        h2.docs-title Use NeoAdmin Skills in Another Repository
        p Use this when another application needs NeoAdmin's visual patterns but should keep its own source, routing, and architecture.
        ol
          li Copy only the relevant skill folders from NeoAdmin's <code>.agents/skills/</code> into the host repository's <code>.agents/skills/</code> directory.
          li Keep the host repository's own rules and architecture in its native project instructions.
          li Ask its agent to use a copied NeoAdmin skill for the specific task.
          li Copy and adapt only the required theme assets and semantic Pug examples for the host framework.
        p.mb-0 Do not copy NeoAdmin's complete agent rules or repository context into an unrelated application. The skills describe NeoAdmin conventions; the host repository still owns its architecture and lifecycle.

        h2.docs-title Build the Application or Reuse the Theme
        h5 Standalone NeoAdmin site
        p Clone the repository, run <code>npm install</code> and <code>npm run dev</code>, customize the source under <code>src/</code>, then run <code>npm run build</code>. Deploy the generated <code>docs/</code> directory.
        h5 Existing React, Vue, or Angular application
        p Build NeoAdmin, copy <code>docs/assets/css/neoadmin.css</code> and only the needed icons, fonts, images, and optional integrations into the host project's public assets. Adapt the generated HTML or Pug examples into JSX/TSX, Vue templates, or Angular templates, and update asset paths. Load the theme once; let the host application own routing and component lifecycle.
        p React, Vue, and Angular components, adapters, and project generators are not included. <code>npm run build:vue</code> only emits a framework-free ES module with automatic initialization disabled; it does not provide Vue components.

        h2.docs-title Official Documentation
        ul
          li
            a(href="https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/memory", target="_blank", rel="noopener") Claude Code project memory
          li
            a(href="https://antigravity.google/docs/rules/", target="_blank", rel="noopener") Antigravity Rules
          li
            a(href="https://antigravity.google/docs/skills?app=antigravity-ide", target="_blank", rel="noopener") Antigravity Agent Skills
          li
            a(href="https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/blob/main/docs/cli/gemini-md.md", target="_blank", rel="noopener") Gemini CLI project context
          li
            a(href="https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/blob/main/docs/cli/using-agent-skills.md", target="_blank", rel="noopener") Gemini CLI Agent Skills
          li
            a(href="https://developers.openai.com/cookbook/examples/codex/iterating-development-workflows-with-codex", target="_blank", rel="noopener") Codex project instructions and skills
          li
            a(href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/prompting-and-context/custom-instructions", target="_blank", rel="noopener") GitHub Copilot custom instructions

        p.mb-0
          a(href="docs.html") Return to NeoAdmin Documentation
